A||||35||KissMySSFiero||Where do you see fieros in Ten years?||||1||
Z||000000||KissMySSFiero||09-29-2001||12:50 PM||||I am bored and wondered what other people think will happen with your fieros.  i know everyone will say they will keep it forever.  but what happens when it hits 300,000 miles and just wears out.  will the value go up to the point where you cant get one?  will they all be gone from existance?  will all the junkyards crush them within days of getting any that might come in?  what about after market.  will it be like the 50's era chevy where you can get brand new after markey panels for next to nothing?  or will all after market stuff be ferrari look-a-like parts?  will we rely on fiero only way overpriced junkyards that have 200 cars but want $200 for a seatbelt because theyre the only place that has them?  <P>who knows....what if in ten years no-one drives cars that run on GAS!!  <P>I think that i think about useless stuff to much.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-29-2001||01:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>...what if in ten years no-one drives cars that run on GAS!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hush your mouth!  Don't say that.  What if they outlaw gasoline?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  On the other hand, I'd have the best looking flower box in town!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||09-29-2001||05:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>"Where do you see Fieros in ten years?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>...........<I>in my driveway!!</I>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Rick 88||09-29-2001||05:15 PM||||Hopefully by that time, GM will have opened their eyes and be producing a modern version of our cars. Until then I'm going to try to keep mine alive and well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||09-29-2001||05:22 PM||||I don't see myself owning my '86SE in ten years, but I see my Formula with me my entire life. It is 3 miles under 180K with original engine(not for long). I figure the new one will last at least another 180K. <P>Internal combustion engine will never be gone in our life-time(if ever). There is no way we could function with something else, think of the costs to replace with something else. I would like to see hydrogen fuel-cells become more commonplace(electric cars are the future my a$$).<P>The concensus seems to be around 150K Fieros on the roads today. Take into account accidents, and other car killing events, and in 10 years, I don't think there will be one at most every corner like todays world. Parts will still be available, and some will still be used for daily driver. I don't see a whole lot changing for them. Unfortunately(IMHO) the Fiero has gained LOTS of popularity, and people are buying them up, and driving or stripping them(I considered the Fiero "my secret" a few years ago). Now everyone wants one.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-29-2001||05:55 PM||||I think they are having their last glory already. In ten years I expect there will be bloody few on the road.<P>As many people like them now, it's simply a matter of attrision. It's already becoming hard to find allot of parts beyond what is common to other cars using the same engines. <P>Salvage yards are cruching them almost as fast as they are dragged in. They are considered a trash car and not worth the space to hold them for stripping.<P>I may be soon to have access to some small space for stripping a few cars but unless allot of people take more interest in salvaging parts the day will come soon where no-one will be able to find anything. When you can find it you won't be able to afford it. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||I wish I had a GT||09-29-2001||06:42 PM||||One of two things will happen,  fiero will become a collectors car, very high demand to a select few people (seat belts $200 hehe)<P>Or they will considered completely worthless.....I'm leaning twords the first scenario though||||reg||1
Z||000007||grgoyl86||09-29-2001||08:21 PM||||With all the engines avaialble now (stock and swaps), someone who has a real desire to can keep a Fiero on the road for a long time.<BR>Also, suspension pieces are available, at least for the 84-87 models. And, for those who have seen inside the barn at Kick Hill Farm, there are LOTS of used parts available there!<P>Having said all that, I want to add that I have been through many types of vehicles, and have tired of each of them with about two years. Well, I bought a Fiero in 1993, and have not had a desire to get anything else since. We (my wife loves hers also) have had several Fieros, and I have had the opportunity to acquire several parts cars. Most of these are cars that are too tired to continue, so they have become "organ donors" so that other Fieros may have a better life.<P>It seems that every time I go to a show, be it a Fiero Show, or a Pontiac show with a bunch of Fieros, the quality of the Fieros there just gets better and better.<P>I like ALL Fieros. I have a GT that I show, and Penny and I each have four-cyl coupes that we drive every day. Hers has about 160,000 miles on it, and the engine is showing signs of old age. So, we now have another engine that we will get rebuilt and have ready to install in there.<P>Keep those Fieros going!<P>Bob||||reg||14
Z||000008||Leper||09-29-2001||09:01 PM||||Chevy Vegas and Dodge Darts became popular again.<BR>Anything is possible.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Leper (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||8Ball||09-29-2001||09:12 PM||||I am always interested in a lot the specializes only in Fieros  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thought about it myself a time or two. Although I could not afford such a thing atm.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cheever3000||09-29-2001||09:35 PM||||I have always proclaimed that somehow we need to preserve the best examples of each stock model, and some customized too, for future generations to marvel at.  Nobody has ever communicated to me any agreement, so I fear they will eventually disappear except for those owned by the few who are able to afford proper storage.  And then almost nobody will be able to see them & appreciate them.<P>But I also realize Fiero owners are not typically the kind to have the resources for doing that.  It would require corporate partnership, such as some help from GM.  Like that'll ever happen.  Maybe other successful car clubs & museums would have some advice.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||CoolBlue87GT||09-29-2001||10:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B>I what other people think will happen with your fieros</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Plan to still have mine. Have no plans to sell it.<P>Hope to still have the mileage low, may need to replace the tires by then, as they'll be 25 years old by then ! (holy cow !)<P>I hope the value will continue to rise as the years go by. Looked at as the 57 chevys, 63 split window Vett, 67 Chevelle, 68 GTO's, T-birds are.<P>Glad were all united in saving the Fiero's as one of best and only mid engine production car ever made.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1cool.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000012||CoolBlue87GT||09-29-2001||11:28 PM||||I've been thinking about this. The Tucker was another car that was years ahead of its time.<P>Just like the Fiero.<P>Preston Tucker was a man of pioneering spirit, ingenuity, and daring, who revolutionized Detroit in the 1940s with his stunning car of tomorrow. It was streamlined, futuristic, and fast - the car every American dreamed of owning, at a price most people could afford" <P>The big wigs killed it before it could get into production. It had a rear air cooled engine, and a third center headlight that turned with the wheel.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deltuck3.jpg"> <P>They made a movie about the Tucker, I thought it was really good.<P>Who knows, maybe they'll make a movie about the birth and death of the Fieros someday.<P>I bet there's alot of dirt that could be dragged up over the idiots that decided it kill them. A movie would really show everyone how stupid Pontiac was in killing them.<P>Years from now ?  Take a look at the Tucker site, they have a club also, maybe we'll be like them ?  <A HREF="http://www.tuckerclub.org/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tuckerclub.org/</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 09-30-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000013||Raydar||09-29-2001||11:32 PM||||I like to compare the Fiero to the Corvair. Both are unconventional, orphaned GM cars. There are still a bunch of loyal Corvair nuts out there. Last Corvair was what? 68 or 69 model?<BR>That's over 30 years ago.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000014||My7Fieros||09-30-2001||02:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B>I've been thinking about this. The Tucker was another car that was years ahead of its time.<P>Just like the Fiero.<P>Preston Tucker was a man of pioneering spirit, ingenuity, and daring, who revolutionized Detroit in the 1940s with his stunning car of tomorrow. It was streamlined, futuristic, and fast - the car every American dreamed of owning, at a price most people could afford" <P>The big wigs killed it before it could get into production. It had a rear air cooled engine, and a third center headlight that turned with the wheel.<P>They made a movie about the Tucker, I thought it was really good.<P>Who knows, maybe they'll make a movie about the birth and death of the Fieros someday.<P>I bet there's alot of dirt that could be dragged up over the idiots that decided it kill them. A movie would really show everyone how stupid Pontiac was in killing them.<P>Years from now ?  Take a look at the Tucker site, they have a club also, maybe we'll be like them ?  <A HREF="http://www.tuckerclub.org/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tuckerclub.org/</A> <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Last I heard they only made 50 Tuckers, and <B>49</B> are still in existance! Thats pretty good in my book!<P><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cheever3000||09-30-2001||02:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Last I heard they only made 50 Tuckers, and <B>49</B> are still in existance!<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>In that website, click "See a Tucker" on the left side.  They list 4 that are known to be destroyed. There are some which they don't even know the whereabouts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Leper||09-30-2001||02:50 AM||||Ida Automotive is building 50 more Tucker replicas with Northstar engines in them.<BR>The replicas use molded urethane body panels that are designed to be an exact fit on an original Tucker. I'd compare [those] to a Fiero.<P>Even if most of the fiero are destroyed {hope not), there might be someone that would be willing to make a replica for future enthusiasts. They could be built with even better parts than the originals, like the cobra replicas, and be even faster than some of the modified Fieros we have now. SO START BRAINWASHING CHILDREN TO DO THAT WHEN THEY GROW UP! (Or anyone else for that matter.)<p>[This message has been edited by Leper (edited 09-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||CoolBlue87GT||09-30-2001||10:00 AM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B> [QUOTE]In that website, click "See a Tucker" They list 4 that are known to be destroyed. There are some which they don't even know the whereabouts.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I actually have seen one here in Florida. I send an e-mail to the web-master about it. The site did not list Florida as having one.<P>It's located in Ft Myers, Fl  in a small museum.  Back in '89, my Dad & I visited it.<P>It was funny, as the museum was in what once was a food store. Driving past it you wouldn't know it was a museum. The building windows are covered. I work in Ft Myers, and will check if it's still there when I get a chance.<P>It was light green color and was in good shape.  Not sure if they used it in the movie. <BR>It looked just like the following. (photo from web site, I have this diecast model)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deltuck4.jpg">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 09-30-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000018||FieroKyle||09-30-2001||12:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leper:<BR><B>Chevy Vegas and Dodge Darts became popular again.<BR>Anything is possible.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Leper (edited 09-29-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In the year 2000.......-Conan O'Brian<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Dave Gunsul||09-30-2001||12:46 PM||||Judging from the response I and other NIFE members i've talked to get from car shows<BR>it seems to me that the Fieros reputation is <BR>slowly but surely going up. More and more people are interested in them and you hear less and less bad comments about them so i think that the Fiero is going to become more and more popular as time gos on. How much more popular is questionable but with this growing popularity the car will continue.<BR>The aftermarket businesses are also growing<BR>for us, just look at all the new products that are out there. With more aftermarket support our cars will outlive us all but<BR>remember that we as Fiero owners are the ultimate example of our cars. We need to show them as much as possible and be at as many car shows that we can so people see that these cars really are worth keeping and don't suffer from the problems that they think. I think it's also very important to take people for rides or let them drive your Fiero so that they can see what a fun little<BR>car it is.<BR>Wow, didn't mean to turn that into such a long speech.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000020||ray b||09-30-2001||01:53 PM||||cult cars like the vairs <BR>not enuff demand to bring high prices<BR>gto's whent up because everyone wanted one<BR>our cars will go up in price but not alot or very quickly<BR>in ten years maybe up 50% over now.5% a year.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula88||09-30-2001||01:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>I like to compare the Fiero to the Corvair. Both are unconventional, orphaned GM cars. There are still a bunch of loyal Corvair nuts out there. Last Corvair was what? 68 or 69 model?<BR>That's over 30 years ago.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, 69 was the last year for the Corvair.  The first ones came out in the Fall of '59 as a '60 model.  So they had a 10 year run instead of the Fiero's 5, but it's got lots more proprietary hardware on it than the Fiero does.  The Corvair survives and even thrives due to a close knit fan base.  No unlike our little forum here.  One big thing CORSA (Corvair Society of America) does is publish member's lists.  You keep one in your car, so if you're traveling and break down, you can look up the nearest Corvair person to help.  I think that would be a grand idea for Fiero enthusiasts, too.  Not as necessary today, but it will be eventually.<P>What gives the Fiero extreme longevity is the number of engine swaps that can keep the drivetrains updated with modern technology.  You can convert to coil-over suspension if you can't find OEM parts - and repro body panels aren't hard to come by.  With the Fiero's space frame, 30 years from now, there could be a market for fiberglass repros of original body panels.  That wouldn't be any harder than all the rebody stuff.  <P>I don't see the Fiero going extinct anytime soon.  We do need to be mindful of the parts that are available now that won't be in the future though.  Like Orief said, we've got to start salvaging as much as possible whenever we find a junked Fiero.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Fiero5||09-30-2001||02:21 PM||||Bob Parker at Kick Hill Farms has so many parts, even he doesn't know at times what he has. His huge 3 story barn is STUFFED with thousands of Fiero only parts. Everything from screws and clips, to hoods and bumpers and everything in between. If you need a part, 9 out of ten times he has it.<BR>As the Fiero and it's parts become harder to find, Bob Parker will only increase his purchasing of damaged/tired out Fieros for parts to keep the rest of them on the road.<P>In ten years, I see as many on the road as we see now. That would be largly due to the fact that many of us (and our groupe is ever growing) are constantly buying more Fieros to fix up and keep and/or sell. But I see possibly more Fieros at shows in ten years than we see now, as more and more people fall in love with them. It's true, the more this current groupe of growing Fiero owners show off our pride and joys, the more chances others will see them and decide that a Fiero is a good car to collect, fix up and show. <P>Steve||||reg||12
Z||000023||Doni Hagan||09-30-2001||03:19 PM||||I don't think the numbers will remain constant in comparison to today. Although there are hardcore enthusiasts out there (like most of us, for example) there are still those to whom a Fiero ain't nothin' but a way of getting from point A to point B.<P>Last year, a 69 Ford Mustang Mach 1 went for $75,000 at a Kruse auction in Vegas. What did they cost new? 3-5K? As the numbers of Fieros decrease, I think (and hope) that the ones you do see will be the cream on the top. Also, one would think the cost of "parts cars" as we know them now will symbiotically rise.  ||||reg||6
Z||000024||GarethWright||10-01-2001||02:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B><BR>What gives the Fiero extreme longevity is the number of engine swaps that can keep the drivetrains updated with modern technology.   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Heck, if they can put a "Mr. Fusion" in a Delorian, why can't we get it?  When the hover conversion comes out, I'll be first in line.<BR>But on a somewhat more serious note, you can do pretty much Anything with a Fiero chassis, Just update the technology as it comes out, and replace parts when they wear out and it will last forever.  Its when people give up on a car that they get rare, and I can't see many of us doing that.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||GarethWright||10-01-2001||02:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B><BR>What gives the Fiero extreme longevity is the number of engine swaps that can keep the drivetrains updated with modern technology.   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Heck, if they can put a "Mr. Fusion" in a Delorian, why can't we get it?  When the hover conversion comes out, I'll be first in line.<BR>But on a somewhat more serious note, you can do pretty much Anything with a Fiero chassis, Just update the technology as it comes out, and replace parts when they wear out and it will last forever.  Its when people give up on a car that they get rare, and I can't see many of us doing that.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||coolcat||10-01-2001||05:52 AM||||I think that with inflation and the number of road worthy Fiero's going down each year,buy the year 2020 you wont be able to get a nice one for under 40,000. Who knows the Fiero is an increasingly popular car and with its bad reputation slowly fading it will probably become a very sought after car...only time will tell. I know that I will always have mine.<P>As for the Tucker, I have been interested in them for a long time.Then about 4 years ago I got to ride in one (the only tuxedo black one made number 23).It was a dream come true.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan9_2.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/eaglflag_1.gif">     Do you know why America picked the eagle as it's national bird? It's because its the only bird that isn't afraid of the storm.||||reg||1
Z||000027||CoolBlue87GT||10-01-2001||07:44 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by coolcat:<BR><B>As for the Tucker,... about 4 years ago I got to ride in one (the only tuxedo black one made number 23).It was a dream come true.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, thats neat. What was it like ? Did it ride good ? Feel like you were back in time ?<P>I've seen the engine, but never heard it run. How did it run, was it quiet ?<P>Okay, enough questions about the Tucker.<p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||coolcat||10-01-2001||01:10 PM||||The Tucker is a AWESOME car. As for the ride quality it felt like riding on a cloud (pretty good suspension).The engine was originally designed as a helicopter engine so as you can imagine its quite loud,but very mean sounding. <BR> <BR>The story is kinda interesting. I went to a local car show just see the Tucker. Well the owner was quite impressed with my knowledge of the car, and buy the fact that out of all the Ferrari's and Lamborghini's, here is this 15 year old kid that can't get enough of his Tucker. I must have spent a good 4-5 hours talking to him. When the car show was closing he asked me if I would like to go for a ride around the block. Of course I said YES.  <P>For about 20 min's I felt on top of the world. There were a couple of guys there that I could tell were really pissed that I got a ride. Anyway when we were pulling out of the parking lot we were almost broadsided buy a lady not paying attention the owner floored it and we took off.It was scarry for a couple of seconds. All I can say is that Tuckers can get up and go....not bad for a car thats 53 years old and is completely original. <P>We drove around for a little while. You wouldn't believe the attention that car gets. When we got back all I could do was smile. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I thanked him and he gave me his number and said that if I ever wanted to go for another ride just give him a call.I never did call maybe I should hmmmmm <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> any way its something I will never forget.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan9_2.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/eaglflag_1.gif">     Do you know why America picked the eagle as it's national bird? It's because its the only bird that isn't afraid of the storm.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Tigger||10-01-2001||01:23 PM||||Wow, that had to been awesome coolcat.  I've always been impressed with the Tucker.  I believe Preston's company and the Tucker name would still be around today if he hadn't made some ill fated mistakes early starting the company.<P>Sorry, to get back on track here.  Ahhh... don't know where I'll see Fieros in the next 10 years.<P>Hopefully not in the junkyards.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Fformula88||10-01-2001||03:39 PM||||As time goes on these cars will still be around, but less and less will be daily drivers.  There are a lot of Fiero people loyal to the cars, and with the support of places like Kick Hill Farms and even the Fiero Store which has come under some heat lately there will be parts available and people restooring their cars.  Pontiac will no longer be selling stuff, and the less common items will disappear from regular autopart stores inventories, but with plenty of junked cars and some new development and manufacturing these things will live on.  ||||reg||1
Z||000031||thomas_l||10-01-2001||04:10 PM||||I don't see much change in the next 10 years. Sure - some new items unique to the Fiero will get scarce, but the production was strong and there are a lot of Fieros in the junkyards and places like Kick Hill or Ed Parks' shop. On top of that, many parts interchange with FWD cars of that era and there are billions of those to harvest. I think the values will only creep up until junkyard parts really get hard to come by.||||reg||1
Z||000032||GTFiero1||10-01-2001||09:23 PM||||fieros will probably be even more popular than ever in 10 year. In my school some people have asked me what do i want for my first car. I always respond "a pontiac Fiero". no matter if they've never seen a fiero or hadn't read a single thing about it they always know what they are. most of them respond "oh, that little car?" and when every one sees a fiero for the first time when i get my GT im sure they will be amazed. thus starting another generation of Fiero enthusiusts<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||6
Z||000033||artherd||10-02-2001||08:31 AM||||Well, I can say for sure that we're going to look back at today as being the sort of 'last golden years' in which we could pick up a Fiero, an 88 car no less!!! for less than ten grand!!! (no, I'm not pulling your leg!)<P>As the crappy fieros (cars that have been grosely neglected, not maintained like the baby Ferraris they are, parked in direct sunlight, etc..) start to die off, what will remain is a smaller number of really nice cars driven by enthusiests (as opposed to those who 'can't afford anything else but hate the car')<P>It will become a privlidge to work on the self-ajusting clutch, or to pull the brakes and replace the whole system with a Ryane/Held 4-piston kit, instead of the chore and PITA it is now.<P><BR>It'll be like owning a DeLorean, only with more colour options  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000034||Butter||10-02-2001||04:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR><B><BR>who knows....what if in ten years no-one drives cars that run on GAS!!  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>There is an electric motor adapter kit for the Fiero. <A HREF="http://www.austinev.org/evalbum/134.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.austinev.org/evalbum/134.html</A> <P>I plan on having a Fiero 10 years from now if I'm still alive.  I expect the Fiero will always have class but it might not be in style.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||DreXteR||10-02-2001||04:56 PM||||In 10 years mine will be in the same place, in my garage with a cover.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Its my weekend-pleaseure toy, nothing less. Have ALL my attention and finacial power(a very very very limited one hehe) to make it run and mantain. Daily drivers will be in extintion, unless have the propiate care, but the rest of them will be babyed and used only for pleasure and show off(killing rice is allowed 2   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ). Could be a good thing to buy the fieros from the yunkyards to save them, place it in a comon place area. By doing that the existance of the fiero will be granted.<P>DreX<p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
